{
  "status": "VERIFIED_FALSE",
  "reason": "Authoritative sources state exact GPs scale as O(N^3) time and O(N^2) memory and are therefore impractical for “a few thousand” training points, but the specific numeric claim that N ≳ 2,000 already implies memory > 32 GB is incorrect: storing an N×N double matrix for N=2,000 requires ~32 MB (8·N^2 bytes), not 32 GB (which would occur around N ≈ 65,536).",
  "evidence_passages": [
    "“Gaussian processes ... have a computational cost which grows cubically with the number of training points and quadratically in memory; in practice standard exact GP implementations are limited to datasets of size up to a few thousand.” — (summary from Rasmussen & Williams / GPML)",
    "“Gaussian processes (GPs) are powerful … but their computational cost grows cubically with the number of training points (O(n^3)) and their memory grows quadratically (O(n^2)), which in practice limits exact GP training to datasets in the low thousands.” — Gardner et al., “Exact Gaussian Processes on a Million Data Points” (arXiv:1903.08114)"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "Rasmussen, C. E. & Williams, C. K. I., Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning (GPML); http://www.gaussianprocess.org/gpml/",
    "Gardner, J. R., Pleiss, G., Weinberger, K. Q., Bindel, D., & Wilson, A. G., Exact Gaussian Processes on a Million Data Points, arXiv:1903.08114 (2019) — https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08114",
    "Wikipedia: Gaussian process — notes complexity O(n^3) time, O(n^2) memory — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaussian_process"
  ]
}

